    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1084">

                <pb facs="0898" n="888" xml:id="pg888"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.3513/6–2849: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Dairen</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Paddock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dairen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-06-28T09:00:00+09:00">June 28, 1949—9 a.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 29—1:17 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>192. Clerk Chao has not gone home last 2 months due fear difficulties with Soviet
                    and CCP police (Contel 40, March 3, repeated 62<anchor n="33" xml:id="d1084-f1"
                    />). Rarely leaves Consulate premises. Now learned for first time his
                    19-year-old son arrested 3 weeks ago and just released from prison. Son was
                    asked give information father’s work at Consulate; warned that unless he told
                    all he knew regarding father’s spying activities he would suffer serious
                    consequences.</p>
                <p>As result pressure used on him in prison, son’s mind affected.</p>
                <p>Sent Nanking 141, repeated Department, Canton 182, Shanghai 159, Moscow 108.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paddock</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
